# Synthetic and Metabolic
#
# From the lac operon to the first synthetic switch and oscillator of 2000, then the assembly methods, the hidden costs of connecting parts, and the flux models used to design metabolism.

#>bioengineering_synthetic
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!bioengineering
#:form #!timeline
#:level #!advanced
#:review #!sourced
#:valence #!boon
#:era #!contemporary

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Synthetic and Metabolic"
 ~#claim: "Synthetic biology began in 2000 with a toggle switch and an oscillator built from repressors, and it learned that parts load each other and burden the host cell they share."
 ~#proof: "Jacob and Monod for repression, Gardner and Elowitz for the first circuits, Engler and Gibson for assembly, Del Vecchio and Ceroni for loading and burden, Orth for flux balance, Ro and Tu for artemisinin."
 ~#door: "Climb the timeline rungs in order, then read the dependency edges, then try the switch in a spreadsheet."
 ~#audience: "advanced students who know transcription and enzymes"
 continuity: "Synthetic and Metabolic | the host cell they share"
 cold_open: "Engineered gene circuits work, but every part they add draws on the host cell they share with everything else."
}

